+### POST `/api/import/score` — Score reading, harmony and form
+
+Accepts one explicitly chosen PDF, PNG, JPEG or WebP as bytes sent to the configured backend. `engine: "local"` (the request default) reads supported vector PDFs without a model key or external provider call. `mode: "chord_symbols"` reads written chords; `mode: "infer_harmony"` analyzes notes, timing and bass. Inference is reviewable interpretation, not verified original harmony. The endpoint does not fetch a URL, read a supplied filesystem path, store uploaded bytes, or import melody performance tracks.
+
+The editor keeps file selection and **Analyze and import**, with a **Use Meta model**
+checkbox instead of Reader choices. It defaults off (`local`); checked means
+`verified`, always with `mode: "infer_harmony"`. Only Analyze submits the request.
+Toggling clears the prior review and aborts an active browser request without
+starting another one. Images require the checkbox and `meta.fullFileScoreImport: true`.
+Printed symbols and N.C.
+are preserved. Browser cancellation suppresses stale results but does not undo
+an external call already sent or its cost.
+
+`verified` sends the original attachment to one Meta request, with a
+150-second timeout, 32,768 output tokens, low reasoning effort, a default $1.50
+request reservation cap and at most 128 written result bars. After admission,
+local reading runs concurrently against the same original. Neither reader sees
+the other's hypotheses. Once both finish, comparison checks bar counts, key, meter, chord readings
+and note evidence. Aligned printed local symbols/N.C. are retained; discrepancies
+become review flags and low confidence. Unequal counts retain the whole local
+reading without merging or truncating bars. Agreement never increases confidence
+or clears an existing review flag. A failed Meta reading reuses the local
+result and returns an explicitly attributed `local_fallback` result when possible,
+including locally readable scores up to 512 bars. Unresolved bars remain editable.
+If neither reader can recover a score, the request fails.
+
+Responses have optional `verification`: `status` (`compared`, `local_unavailable`,
+`unaligned`, `local_fallback`, or `local_only` when a quick local reading exceeded
+Meta's 128-bar limit and no Meta call was made), `localMeasures`, `metaMeasures`, `comparedMeasures` (each up to 512), zero-based
+`disputedMeasures`, and explanatory `notes`. A reading that violates the import
+contract answers 502 with the first violation instead of a server error. An unsupported local file may be read
+by Meta because the checkbox explicitly authorizes that path; it reports zero
+compared bars and marks the result for review. This is not a measured accuracy score.
+Legacy `auto` and `meta` modes remain available to API/MCP clients.
+
+Local `infer_harmony` may use the served `ft_f1` checkpoint for up to eight
+unresolved note-bearing bars in a score of at most 128 bars. These suggestions
+keep `analysisSource: local`, `model: ft_f1`, `requestId: null`, low confidence
+and their original note evidence. Deterministic bars keep `model: null`.
+The model receives known harmony; candidates are compared against fixed later
+chords in written order, within the checkpoint's token window (`max_seq_len`, 256 for the served checkpoints; a longer score is compared through the nearest bars on each side of the passage, reported in the `ranking` label, and refused only when the neighbouring bars themselves do not fit). No mask-trained infilling
+or original-chord recovery is claimed. Unread notation and explicit N.C. are
+never targets. Unknown vocabulary or unavailable weights leave an explicit warning.
+
+For `auto` plus `infer_harmony`, each harmony batch receives the complete ordered
+score synopsis: key/meter, printed versus inferred chords, confidence, N.C., pitch
+classes and known sections, plus target notes and neighboring harmony. All local
+inferred bars are eligible, including high-confidence proposals. At most two
+batches of eight bars and 512 notes each are reviewed concurrently over the same
+frozen synopsis; form analysis follows their joined results. Remaining bars retain their
+local result and are reported as unreviewed, not Meta-analyzed.
+
+One additional form call uses the complete updated synopsis when any bars lack
+section coverage. Existing sections, including null-type source headings, are
+preserved. Up to 32 inferred ranges may fill uncovered passages; uncertain functions
+may have null type and a neutral Section label, and an empty proposal is allowed.
+The initial complete synopsis is checked before spending: at most 512 bars and
+40 KiB, with no truncation. Each serialized assist/form request allows 64 KiB,
+45 seconds, 4096 completion tokens and $0.10. Combined admission is **at most
+three calls / $0.30**. `auto` with `chord_symbols` remains local transcription.
+
+Unsupported local input fails without a Meta request; `auto` never silently sends
+the whole file. Explicit `meta` sends the attachment in one 150-second, 32,768-token
+request with low reasoning effort, a default $1.50 cap and 128 result bars. It returns harmony/form together,
+without the separate form step. On a public host, `meta` and `verified` both
+require `THEARTIST_PUBLIC_META_READER=1` and usable Meta configuration.
+
+```json
+{
+  "filename": "score.pdf",
+  "mimeType": "application/pdf",
+  "dataBase64": "<base64 file bytes, without a data-URL prefix>",
+  "mode": "chord_symbols",
+  "engine": "local"
+}
+```
+
+The filename must be a plain filename matching MIME and file signature. The decoded limit is 8 MiB. Local reading allows 50 pages/512 written measures and bounded page objects, glyphs and note events. Attachment-based Meta reading allows 128 measures. Review allows 16 symbols per measure and 64 non-overlapping sections. Unsupported encoding, unresolved rhythm and octave/voice limitations are reported explicitly.
+
+The JSON response is a **review draft**, not a ready-to-replace editor song:
+
+| Field | Shape and meaning |
+|---|---|
+| `title` | Printed title or an empty string; at most 120 characters |
+| `mode` | Echoes `chord_symbols` or `infer_harmony`; unknown request modes are rejected |
+| `analysisSource` | `local`, `meta` or `mixed`; per-measure `analysisSource` and `model`/`requestId` identify the actual boundary |
+| `key` | Canonical `<root> major/minor` or `null`; aliases such as C/Am normalize to C major/A minor. An inferred or unrecognized key produces a review warning |
+| `timeSignature` | `[numerator, denominator]` or `null`; playback BPM is chosen by the user, not extracted |
+| `measures` | `[{chords, sourceLabel, needsReview, note, chordBasis, confidence, notes, writtenNotes, bassNotes, noChord, timeSignature, analysisSource, model, requestId}]`, in written order. `notes` are sounding pitches, `writtenNotes` retain notation before a resolved octave shift, and `bassNotes` retain actual low-pitch evidence. `noChord` distinguishes explicit N.C. from unreadable emptiness. Source meters remain per measure |
+| `sections` | `[{section, label, startMeasure, length, basis, note}]`; `section` is a supported section id or `null`, indices are zero based, `basis` is `explicit` or `inferred` |
+| `formAnalysis` | Null when the separate form step did not run; otherwise `{model, requestId, sectionsProposed}`, including zero proposals. A full-file response may contain sections while this field is null |
+| `warnings` | Review notes, including any repeat/ending limitations |
+| `model`, `requestId` | `null` for local reading, except that `model` is `ft_f1` (top level and on each completed measure, `requestId` still `null`) when own-model harmonization filled unresolved bars; actual provider metadata when Meta is used. Mixed responses retain request/model metadata per affected measure |
+
+Raw measure chord counts are preserved. The editor opens the editable song as soon
+as key, meter and section metadata are valid (Import reviewed score confirms a
+corrected draft otherwise); unresolved harmony does not require a separate checkbox or block editing. One chord fills both equal
+slots, two fill one each. More than two changes leave the slots unassigned while
+retaining every source proposal; unsupported symbols remain unresolved. Repeats,
+D.C./D.S. and endings remain in written order. An unsupported or declared incomplete
+full-file transcription is rejected rather than silently truncated.
+
+Inferred chords require nonempty visible-note/bass evidence or an explicit melody-only rationale in `note`, and always have `needsReview: true`. Low confidence and unreadable measures also require review. Missing inference provenance is rejected; the printed-symbol mode rejects a provider result that claims inferred chords. The score editor shows chord/Roman labels and selected-bar source evidence. Saved sections retain explicit/inferred/manual origin, and project metadata retains source proposals and attribution, never uploaded file bytes.
+
+See [score analysis](docs/score-analysis.md) for algorithms, encoding and confidence limits, and the [real import evaluation](experiments/import_eval/README.md) for exact requests/results, including the PNG reading failure. Source meter changes remain evidence while the editor plays/exports one global meter. Successful transport and high model confidence do not establish optical or song-form accuracy.
+
+Service errors use `{"detail": "..."}`; request-schema errors use FastAPI's validation-error list. Statuses are 400 for invalid filename/base64; 403 full-file reading on a public host; 413 attachment too large; 415 unsupported/mismatched format; 422 unsupported local reading, oversized complete synopsis or invalid/incomplete extraction; 429 API budget limit; 502 invalid provider response; 503 missing/invalid configuration or ledger, or `THEARTIST_META_ENABLED=0` (auto fails when a Meta call is needed, with no silent local fallback); 504 Meta timeout. Provider-output validation errors do not echo extracted score text.
+
+#### POST `/api/import/score/stream`
+
+Same body as `/api/import/score`; the response is `text/event-stream` (`Cache-Control: no-cache, no-transform`, `X-Accel-Buffering: no`). Frames: `progress` `{"label": "..."}` for each reader stage (file check, local reading, Meta reading, comparison), a comment keep-alive line (`: reading score`) every 5 s while a reader is busy, then exactly one terminal frame: `result` carrying the JSON response described above, or `error` `{"detail": "...", "status": <one of the HTTP statuses listed above>}`. The editor's **Analyze and import** uses this variant. Closing the stream does not cancel a Meta call already sent; its cost still settles.
+
+### POST `/api/export/midi` — Edited arrangement to MIDI
+
+This endpoint renders the current musical state, without generating new chords. The editor and external clients share the strict `ExportSpec` model:
+
+```json
+{
+  "title": "Sketch",
+  "bpm": 96,
+  "key": "C major",
+  "timeSignature": [4, 4],
+  "genre": "pop",
+  "measures": [
+    {"chords": [{"symbol": "C/E", "voicing": [52, 55, 60]}, null]}
+  ],
+  "sections": [{"label": "A", "startMeasure": 0, "length": 1}],
+  "play": {
+    "harmonyOverride": null,
+    "harmonyRhythmId": "block",
+    "bassEnabled": true,
+    "drumMode": "auto",
+    "drumPatternId": null,
+    "masterVolume": 1,
+    "harmonyVolume": 1,
+    "bassVolume": 1,
+    "drumVolume": 0.6
+  }
+}
+```
+
+All displayed fields are required. Unknown fields and invalid types are rejected; clients send numeric values as JSON numbers, not strings.
+
+| Field | Constraints |
+|---|---|
+| `title`, `bpm`, `key` | Title at most 120 characters; integer BPM 20–300; key is a note name followed by `major` or `minor` |
+| `timeSignature` | Numerator 1–32; denominator one of 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32. MIDI tempo uses quarter notes; timing scales to the actual denominator. |
+| `genre` | String up to 40 characters or `null`; supplies Auto instrument/rhythm/drum defaults |
+| `measures` | 1–512 measures, each with exactly two chord slots; `null` is an empty slot |
+| Non-null chord | `symbol` of 1–80 characters with a valid root/slash bass; `voicing` of 1–16 integer MIDI pitches, each 0–127 |
+| `sections` | At most 128 ranges, non-overlapping and contained in the measures; positive length, zero-based start; labels of 1–80 characters |
+| `harmonyOverride`, `harmonyRhythmId` | Known instrument/preset or `null` for Auto |
+| `bassEnabled` | Boolean; `false` omits the bass track |
+| `drumMode`, `drumPatternId` | `auto` uses the genre; `manual` uses a known pattern id or `null` for drums off |
+| Four volume fields | Finite numbers in 0–1; `0` is preserved as a mute |
+
+`voicing` contains the notes the editor actually plays after its piano/guitar resolver. `visualMode` is not part of this wire format. This preserves selected voicings and slash basses without asking the backend to reconstruct them from a chord label.
+
+Success returns **binary** `audio/midi`, with an attachment filename and `Cache-Control: no-store`. The type-1 SMF uses 480 PPQ: metadata track (title, tempo, time signature, key, section markers), Harmony channel 0, optional Bass channel 1, and optional Drums channel 9. Notes follow the selected rhythm, two-chord boundaries and swing, and are cut before another note of the same pitch/channel starts. Mixer levels become per-part CC7 values multiplied by Master; a muted enabled part still has a track with CC7=0. A DAW's selected sound library and treatment of MIDI controls can change the audible result.
+
+The render is limited to 100,000 notes and 4 MiB. Invalid export data returns 422 with a field location; excessive render size returns 413. This route writes no server-side file. PDF export is a separate browser-rendered score document and does not use this endpoint.
+
+### POST `/api/export/scripter` — Logic Pro Scripter script
+
+The same `ExportSpec` body as `/api/export/midi`. The response (`text/javascript; charset=utf-8`, `Content-Disposition: attachment; filename*=UTF-8''<title>.theartist.js`, `Cache-Control: no-store`) is a Logic Pro Scripter MIDI FX script that carries the song's harmony and bass notes — the same notes, quarter-note times and velocities the MIDI export writes — and plays them inside Logic following its transport, locate and cycle. Drums stay with the MIDI file or Logic's own drummer. The script works on the song form, not Logic's open timeline: its **Section** menu plays the whole form or one section of `spec.sections` from the chosen **Start bar**, and it is silent beyond that. Other parameters: Transpose (±12), Harmony and Bass on/off, Velocity %, Loop (the chosen section or the whole form), and Beat unit (Logic's ruler counts the meter's denominator as one beat).
+
+In Logic: MIDI FX ▸ Scripter ▸ Open Script in Editor, paste the file, Run Script. Errors match the MIDI route: 422 for an invalid spec, 413 over the note limit or over 4 MiB of script. See [plugin/logic-scripter/README.md](plugin/logic-scripter/README.md).
+
+### GET `/api/daw/targets` and POST `/api/daw/open` — Local handoff
+
+`GET /api/daw/targets` returns the backend machine's installed applications:
+
+```json
+{"targets":[
+  {"id":"logic","name":"Logic Pro","available":true,"action":"open"},
+  {"id":"ableton","name":"Ableton Live","available":false,"action":"reveal"}
+]}
+```
+
+On non-macOS both targets are unavailable. `POST /api/daw/open` takes `{"target":"logic"|"ableton","spec":<ExportSpec>}` and returns `{"action":"open"|"reveal","path":"<saved MIDI path>"}`. It writes a uniquely named MIDI under `backend/.exports/`, then opens it in Logic or reveals it in Finder for dragging into Ableton. It does not insert tracks directly into an existing DAW project.
+
+This is an explicit side effect on the **backend computer**. Keep this local
+backend bound to loopback. Hosted clients can download `.mid` or use the separate
+[local Mac bridge](docs/midi-input.md#open-a-hosted-score-in-logic-pro) for Logic;
+the cloud server never launches desktop applications. Errors are 409 for a missing
+application, 501 on non-macOS, and 500 for file/open failures (an already saved
+file remains available). Export validation/size errors remain 422/413.
+
+### Judge-server lockdown and Meta spend limits
+
+Off by default. `THEARTIST_PUBLIC_HOST=1`: `GET /api/daw/targets`, `POST /api/daw/open` and `POST /api/models/warmup` answer 403 with a reason, and `/mcp/` is not mounted. `POST /api/import/score` (and `/stream`) refuses both whole-file reads, `engine: "meta"` and `engine: "verified"` (the editor's Use Meta model checkbox), with 403 unless `THEARTIST_PUBLIC_META_READER=1`; `"auto"` and `"local"` stay. Enabling the reader does not disable Meta's master switch, Standard model requirement or spend limits. A session allowance must accommodate the $1.50 full-file reservation. The Meta-calling routes — planner runs with `planner: "meta"`, Second-ear reviews, Reharmonize, and a score import that is about to call Meta — are then admitted against limits (also available alone with `THEARTIST_META_LIMITS=1`):
+
+| Header | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| `X-TheArtist-Session` | Required unless a valid `X-TheArtist-Judge-Code` is sent; a stable per-browser id (≤ 128 characters). Its pool allows `THEARTIST_META_SESSION_LIMIT_USD` per UTC day (default 1.00). |
+| `X-TheArtist-Judge-Code` | Optional; equal to `THEARTIST_JUDGE_CODE`, it draws from one shared judge pool of `THEARTIST_JUDGE_CODE_DAILY_USD` (default 5.00). The Pages proxy forwards only `Accept`, `Content-Type` and `X-TheArtist-Session`, so the judge code is honoured only against the model API origin. |
+
+A request is admitted at its worst case: planner $0.10, review $0.03,
+Reharmonize $0.15, full-file score reading $1.50, or combined harmony/form assist $0.30. An auto import requiring
+no Meta call is not charged. Settlement counts only the request's own calls,
+including retries and failures; it never subtracts global ledger snapshots that
+could include other callers. Import collects its multiple calls inside the
+synchronous request worker's context. Failed ledger settlement retains the
+import admission estimate; planner/review errors retain the known paid cost.
+The global cap `THEARTIST_META_DAILY_LIMIT_USD` (default 10.00) counts the UTC day's
+ledger entries. Statuses: 400 missing or over-long session header, 403 wrong judge
+code, 429 a limit reached (with `Retry-After` to UTC midnight), 503 a misconfigured
+limit variable or unreadable ledger. A 429's detail names the pool and the reset time
+but no amount; the figures go to the server log. Reharmonize does not answer the
+limiter's 429. It runs its search without Meta instead and returns 200 with
+`selection: "local"` and `metaUnavailable: "limit"`; the limiter's 503 likewise
+becomes a search without Meta (`paused` for an unreadable ledger, `unconfigured` for a
+refused setting). A search that will not call Meta asks for no admission. A refused
+setting is named without its value.
+
+### GET `/api/capabilities` and `/mcp/` — Shared contract discovery
+
+`GET /api/capabilities` returns `{"schemaVersion":"1.0","data":{...}}`, including implemented tools, contract names, payload limits, source/model fields and known limitations. The MCP `capabilities` tool returns the same envelope. The `data.rag` object reports `enabled`, `ready`, `sources` (`omt` and `berklee`: `ready`, `missing`, `invalid` or `disabled`), `method: "local_tfidf"` and `notes: "source_excerpt"`. A bad source configuration returns `error: "invalid_source_configuration"`. Other REST responses keep their existing shapes; they are not wrapped in this envelope.
+
+`data.meta` includes `enabled`, `configured`, `available`, `unavailableReason`,
+`creditStop`, `provider`, `model`, `judge` and `fullFileScoreImport`. `creditStop` is
+true while a `THEARTIST_META_CREDIT_USD` line is set (a value that is not a number counts,
+since it refuses every call) and never carries the value. `available` is false while
+Meta controls should be off, and `unavailableReason` says why. `disabled` means the
+flag is off or invalid. `unconfigured` means there is no key, or a configuration the
+client refuses (logged once on the server). `paused` covers three cases: less than
+$0.05 is left on the `THEARTIST_META_CREDIT_USD` credit stop line or, with dollar
+caps on, under `THEARTIST_META_BUDGET_USD`; the ledger cannot be read; or the
+provider answered within the last 10 minutes that the account has no credit. The
+block never reports a spend or credit amount.
+
+While the credit line or the provider pause holds, every call is refused before any
+network request with HTTP 503 (`kind: "disabled"` in planner and review errors), and
+Reharmonize answers without Meta. A call is also refused whenever its own worst case
+would pass the line. The dollar cap keeps its own 429. `judge` is `{available,
+unavailableReason}` for the judge role that Second ear runs on, which can be another
+provider. `fullFileScoreImport` is true only while Meta is available on a Standard
+`muse-spark-1.3` provider and what the line and cap still allow covers a whole-file
+read's worst case (about $1.45). `THEARTIST_PUBLIC_META_READER=1` is additionally
+required on a public host. Clients treat a missing `fullFileScoreImport` as false,
+and a missing `available` as `enabled && configured` (a server that predates it). Import limits advertise `scoreMeasures: 512`, `metaScoreMeasures: 128`,
+`scoreAssistHarmonyBars: 16`, `scoreAssistCalls: 3`, `scoreAssistRunUsd: 0.30`, and
+source fields include `sections.*.basis` and `formAnalysis`.
+
+The Streamable HTTP endpoint is `/mcp/` on the backend, outside the `/api` prefix. It exposes 11 tools: `capabilities`, `list_models`, `list_genres`, `get_genre_preset`, `generate_section`, `plan_song`, `validate_plan`, `build_song`, `render_midi`, `import_score` and `export_daw`. REST and MCP call the same generation, planning, import and export implementations. There is no theory explanation tool, and the Second-ear review and Reharmonize (`POST /api/reharmonize`) are REST only.
+
+Successful MCP results put the versioned envelope in `structuredContent`. Clients must check `isError`, `schemaVersion` and documented size limits, and preserve `source`/`model_used` fields. `import_score` additionally requires explicit attachment confirmation. MCP DAW handoff requires a direct loopback request, server opt-in and explicit per-call confirmation. See [the MCP contract and thin-client example](docs/mcp.md) for exact arguments, safeguards and transport setup.
 
